  "excluded_prefixes": {
    "plugins/hosted/directory/generated/": "The same render output, one directory over: the generated channel packets embed the digests above and are rewritten by the same automation. An earlier round pinned them on the ground that they carry v1's release identity, which was true and still is -- but they carry it derived, and a derived copy of a machine-owned value is not a source byte a human can silently change.",
    "plugins/hosted/directory/publication/": "Operator-driven listing state. It records where a channel submission currently sits in a marketplace review queue, which moves without any release changing, so pinning it would make an operator action look like a v1-v4 mutation.",
    "plugins/hosted/directory/submissions/": "One file per submission attempt, appended by the operator as channels are re-submitted. Additive by design, and the same argument applies.",
    "plugins/hosted/generated/": "Machine-owned render output. `.github/workflows/release-please.yml` runs `hosted-plugin.py regenerate`/`render` over this tree and commits the result with `git add plugins/hosted`, so every release that moves the schema contract rewrites these locks and descriptors. Pinning them made a released main red on this guard, with a message that read as a release-identity violation. What the pin would catch here -- a feature PR silently altering a generated package -- is already caught by `hosted-plugin.py check` freshness and the release parity gate, which recompute the bytes rather than remembering them."
  },
